                                         Chapter II – Theoretical Background 
 
 
The development of the CitySamadhan system is based on fundamental concepts 
of web application development, including client-server architecture, database 
management, and modern web technologies. In a client-server model, the user 
interacts with the system through a web browser (client), which sends requests to the 
server. The server processes these requests, interacts with the database, and returns 
the required response, ensuring efficient and centralized data handling. 
 
The system uses a Database Management System (DBMS) to store and manage 
complaint data. Technologies like SQLite or PostgreSQL are integrated with 
SQLAlchemy ORM, which simplifies database operations by allowing interaction 
through Python objects instead of complex SQL queries. The backend is developed 
using the Flask framework, a lightweight Python-based web framework that 
supports rapid development and modular design. On the frontend, HTML, CSS, and 
JavaScript are used to create a responsive and user-friendly interface. 
 
Additionally, the system incorporates geolocation services to capture the location 
of complaints and email notifications to keep users updated. These concepts 
collectively ensure that the system is efficient, scalable, and suitable for real-world 
civic complaint management. 
